  "worked_solution": "First, we find the constant $D.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4x+3 & =2x(x+2)+D({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)^{2}+4(-2)+3 & =2(-2)(-2+2)+D((-2)^{2}+3) \\\\ 7 & =7D \\\\ D & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+3=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x+2|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+4x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+3)+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
